<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no" ?>
<FinalDraft DocumentType="Script" Template="No" Version="1">

  <Content>
    <Paragraph Alignment="Center" Type="Action">
      <Text>CHARACTERS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>FATAK - a boy of twelve, who has never known a day without biryani
</Text>
      <Text>AMMA - his mother
</Text>
      <Text>RAVI - his friend, competitive, easily wounded by an unfair fielding order
</Text>
      <Text>MEENA - his friend, dry-witted, notices everything
</Text>
      <Text>GOPAL - watchman at the compound next door, unhurried, entirely without self-pity</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Alignment="Center" Type="Action">
      <Text>SETTING</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A quiet residential lane, and the homes that face each other across it. The present day, or near enough.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Alignment="Center" Type="Action">
      <Text>ACT ONE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. FATAK'S HOUSE - KITCHEN - DAY</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A modest kitchen, warm with the smell of spices. A large pot simmers on the stove. AMMA, early forties, stirs it with the ease of long habit.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>FATAK, twelve, hovers in the doorway, craning his neck toward the pot.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Is it ready, Amma? It smells ready.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMMA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(not turning around)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It smells ready every day. That doesn't mean it is.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Today it smells more ready than usual.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMMA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(a small laugh)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Wash your hands. By the time you're back it'll be on the table.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak doesn't move. He edges closer, peering into the pot.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Chicken biryani?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMMA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>When is it ever anything else, in this house.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(grinning)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Just checking.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He hurries off. A beat later he's back, sliding into his chair as AMMA sets down a heaped plate -- rice glistening with ghee, dark with spice, a single piece of chicken resting on top like a small trophy.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(closing his eyes, inhaling)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Every single day, and it still smells like the first time.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMMA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That's because I make it fresh. Not everyone gets that.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(already eating)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Lucky me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They sit in comfortable quiet as Fatak eats -- not fast, not slow, the unhurried appetite of a boy who has never once had to wonder if there will be more.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMMA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Leave room for your tea.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(mouth full)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>There's always room for tea.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A little later. The plate is empty. AMMA sets a small glass of tea in front of him, steam curling upward. Fatak wraps both hands around it, entirely content.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Best part of the day.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMMA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You say that about the biryani too.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(considering this seriously)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's a very good day. Every day.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He drains the last of the tea, sets the glass down with satisfaction, and heads off, schoolbag over one shoulder, already thinking of something else. This has happened every day for longer than either of them can properly remember.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. FATAK'S HOUSE - STREET - EVENING</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Golden light slants across a quiet lane. FATAK sits on the compound step, bouncing a tennis ball against the wall and catching it, again and again. Nothing to do, and enjoying it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>RAVI and MEENA round the corner. RAVI drags an old cricket bat behind him like a walking stick. MEENA carries three stumps under one arm.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAVI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Fatak! Up, up, we're one man short.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(springing up)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Where's Sameer?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MEENA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Tuition. His mother is very serious about fractions.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAVI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(planting the stumps against the wall)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Which means we need you. Catch practice first -- Sameer's not here to bowl.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I'm always fielding first.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAVI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Because you're always late.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I was eating.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MEENA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(deadpan)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You are always eating.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(entirely unbothered)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Biryani doesn't wait for cricket. Cricket waits for biryani.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MEENA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(tossing the ball to Ravi)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Loosen up first. You always throw wild when you skip that part.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAVI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(already winding up)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I'm warmed up.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He is not warmed up. He throws hard, aiming for Meena -- the ball sails high, wide, over the compound wall next door, and disappears.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A beat of stunned silence.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MEENA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That was Mr. Ilyas's compound.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAVI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(losing enthusiasm fast)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That was Mr. Ilyas's compound.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They all look at Fatak.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Why is everyone looking at me?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAVI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You're standing closest to the gate.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak looks at the gate. Looks back at his friends.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(sighing, already walking)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Fine. But if there's a dog in there, I'm blaming both of you at the funeral.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He pushes through the half-open gate and disappears from view. RAVI and MEENA wait, unconcerned, already arguing about whether a lost ball cancels the throw.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Alignment="Center" Type="Action">
      <Text>END OF ACT ONE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Alignment="Center" Type="Action">
      <Text>ACT TWO</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. ILYAS COMPOUND - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A smaller, plainer yard. Washing hangs on a line. In the far corner, in the day's last stretch of shade, GOPAL -- fifties, wiry, the compound's watchman -- sits on an upturned crate. A steel plate rests on his knees: a small mound of plain white rice, one green chilli, nothing else.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He eats slowly. Unhurried. Each mouthful measured, as if he is making it last on purpose.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>FATAK enters, scanning the ground, doesn't see him at first.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(to himself)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It has to be somewhere...</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He spots the ball -- rolled to a stop near Gopal's feet -- and stops short, seeing him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Gopal looks up, unbothered, faintly amused by the intrusion.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>GOPAL</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Looking for this?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He nudges the ball toward Fatak with his foot, without rising, without breaking the rhythm of his meal.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(quietly)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Yes. Sorry. My friend threw it a bit too well.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>GOPAL</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(a small smile)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It happens. That wall has seen worse.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak bends to pick up the ball but doesn't leave. He's looking at the plate. The single chilli. The rice, going down mouthful by careful mouthful.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>GOPAL (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(noticing, not unkindly)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Go on. Your friends are waiting.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(a beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Is that -- all you're having?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>GOPAL</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(shrugging, without a trace of self-pity)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's enough. Rice fills you up, if you eat it slowly.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He says it plainly, like a fact about the weather, and takes another unhurried mouthful.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(not sure what to say)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Okay. Um -- thank you. For the ball.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>GOPAL</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(waving him off, kind)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Go. Play.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak lingers a second longer than he means to, then turns and walks back toward the gate, ball in hand, moving slower than he came in.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. FATAK'S HOUSE - STREET - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>RAVI and MEENA are still arguing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAVI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It has to count. The ball left the field of play at maximum velocity --</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MEENA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The ball left the field of play into somebody's dinner. That's not a rule anywhere.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They notice Fatak has returned. He hands Ravi the ball without a word.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAVI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(taking it)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Finally. Okay -- who's batting --</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(quiet)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I think I'm done for today.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MEENA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(surprised)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You? Done playing? Before dinner?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(half a smile, distracted)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>See you tomorrow.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He walks back toward his house. RAVI and MEENA exchange a look, a shrug, and go back to arguing about the throw, already forgetting.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. FATAK'S HOUSE - FATAK'S ROOM - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak lies on his back, eyes open, staring at the ceiling fan turning lazily above him. The house is quiet.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He turns onto his side. Turns back. Sits up.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(to himself, barely audible)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's just rice.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He lies back down. The fan turns. He is very clearly not falling asleep.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(quieter still)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>One chilli.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A long moment. Just the fan, and the boy, wide awake.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Alignment="Center" Type="Action">
      <Text>END OF ACT TWO</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Alignment="Center" Type="Action">
      <Text>ACT THREE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. FATAK'S HOUSE - KITCHEN - DAY (THE NEXT DAY)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The same table. The same plate -- rice glistening with ghee, a piece of chicken resting on top. AMMA sets it down the way she has every day for as long as Fatak can remember.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMMA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Careful, it's hot.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak sits. Looks at the plate. Doesn't move.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMMA (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(pausing, noticing)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>What?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Nothing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMMA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You've stared at that plate for a full minute. That has never once happened in your life.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak picks up his spoon. Sets it down again.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Amma. Can I ask you something.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMMA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(sitting across from him, giving him her full attention)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Always.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Yesterday, when the ball went into Mr. Ilyas's compound -- I saw Gopal eating. Just rice. And one chilli.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMMA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That's often what Gopal has. It's not new.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's new to me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A silence. Amma doesn't rush to fill it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I've sat here twelve years eating this, every single day, and it never once occurred to me to wonder what anyone else was eating. Not once.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMMA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That doesn't make you a bad person.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I know it doesn't. But I can't just eat like yesterday didn't happen. I keep doing the maths in my head and none of it fixes anything. Feeling bad about my plate doesn't make his any bigger.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMMA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>No. It doesn't.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(the decision arriving as he says it)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>So I'm going to eat half. Starting today. Half of what I usually eat means the same biryani lasts twice as long. I don't fully know yet what that changes. But it has to change something.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMMA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(quiet)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And if it doesn't change anything for Gopal?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(a beat, honest)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then it changes something in me. I don't want to be someone who watches a man eat one chilli and forgets by dinner.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Amma looks at her son for a long moment. Then, without another word, she reaches over and moves half the rice from his plate back into the serving dish.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak watches her do it. Doesn't stop her.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That's still a good plate.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMMA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(throat tight, a small smile)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's a very good plate.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He picks up his spoon and eats -- slower than usual. Not because there's less. Because for the first time, he's paying attention.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. FATAK'S HOUSE - STREET - EVENING (WEEKS LATER)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>RAVI and MEENA set the stumps against the compound wall. FATAK approaches, ball in hand -- the same ball.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAVI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You're batting first today. "Fielding first" is clearly rigged against you, we're retiring it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(tossing the ball, catching it)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Deal.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Meena glances toward the gate next door, where -- distantly, through the gap -- GOPAL can be seen settling onto his crate with his evening plate.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MEENA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(quietly, to Fatak)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Sameer says you take forever over lunch now. Is that why?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(a small shrug, not making a thing of it)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I just started paying attention. That's all.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAVI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(already bored of the topic, bat raised)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Can we play, or is this a serious conversation.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(grinning, tossing him the ball)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We can play.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak takes his position. Behind him, through the gate, Gopal eats -- unhurried, same as always. Fatak doesn't look back at him. He doesn't need to, anymore. He already knows.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The game begins.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Alignment="Center" Type="Action">
      <Text>END OF PLAY</Text>
    </Paragraph>
  </Content>
</FinalDraft>
